#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486489} :activity/id #uuid "b133af0b-64de-5a69-afba-ce5e85bd85f5" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048552 :topic/id #uuid "a92f5097-81a3-51a2-90e4-397763d967f0" :topic/math-academy-id 1909} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486489 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486458 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26aab52f-ff38-5268-9e76-730ae0da1260" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485830} {:answer/id #uuid "2c393b44-730a-5f93-9acb-fbe9996ca594" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485842} {:answer/id #uuid "4ebabf65-87a9-50c0-a00e-9f63618f09de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485911} {:answer/id #uuid "99988b28-0eb3-5cc7-aaa9-88164b97c763" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486040} {:answer/id #uuid "9ff9d9bd-25c1-553b-af23-b5a3804fa12a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486051}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486051} :answer-field/id #uuid "399a1def-da95-5c7b-b07c-dd77153c4399" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486258}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f5e736f9-a7fd-5964-86e8-9e7f4c30d97a" :question/math-academy-id "q-35254" :question/problem "Let $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ and $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$ be bases of ${R}^{3}$ and ${R}^{2}$, respectively. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2},x\\}$, where $x\\in {R}^{3}$, is a basis of ${R}^{2}$\n\nII. $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},4{b}_{3}\\}$ is a basis of ${R}^{3}$\n\nIII. $\\{{c}_{2},{c}_{1}\\}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "Since $B$ and $C$ are bases, the following properties must hold:\n\n- $B$ and $C$ are maximally linearly independent sets in ${R}^{2}$ and ${R}^{3},$ respectively, and\n\n- $B$ spans ${R}^{2},$ while $C$ spans ${R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The set $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2},x\\}$ is not a basis of ${R}^{2}$ because the set contains vectors from another vector space.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+{x}_{3}(4{b}_{3}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+(4{x}_{3}){b}_{3} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, since $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent, gives\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 4{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},4{b}_{3}\\}$ spans $V$ since any linear combination of vectors from $B$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},4{b}_{3}\\}:$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+{x}_{3}{b}_{3}={x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+\\frac{{x}_{3}}{4}(4{b}_{3})\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. The set $\\{{c}_{2},{c}_{1}\\}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$ because the set consists of all the vectors from the basis $C$ written in a different order.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."} :step/id #uuid "7517e0b5-948e-589e-82bc-4f03c404dd2f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486522}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486493 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244046 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd6a732f-6cdd-5655-a6d6-50c4868da54c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447920} {:answer/id #uuid "5fbf8f5f-a4a5-551f-94f1-dcb9e98cce3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447921}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447920} :answer-field/id #uuid "7be9815c-003c-59b0-bf27-d84a02bc7230" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449000} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28275147-4f3e-5c35-a4b7-26449bd54feb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447922} {:answer/id #uuid "4df74a3a-11fc-54e3-9232-cbadde994cdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447923}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447923} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a7adf00-543a-53ac-bd3f-338d162d18d1" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449001} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07492db5-983b-53e0-8d42-a96b1163f4df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447924} {:answer/id #uuid "7100bd53-8f94-5723-9f27-437f2039702e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447925}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447924} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5f0bf28-1c7e-5b15-aea9-897d2c7a4160" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449002}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "265a681e-3e0d-553b-975a-40472b8deb57" :question/math-academy-id "q-107347" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]}$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n- **II.** $[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}]$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]}$\n- **III.** ${[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]}$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that\n\n$$\n1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+0\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]+0\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere the first coefficient is nonzero, so the set is linearly dependent. In general, any set that contains a zero-matrix is linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]=0\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]+(-3)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the set is not linearly independent, it can't form a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "863020c7-988a-55d4-89e2-3c40430f3b22" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486497}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486497 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244069 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c503b3e-b3ee-58f2-b568-8feac45ab65f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447926} {:answer/id #uuid "18ceae87-0b3a-552a-904a-8aa94f6b3988" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447927}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447927} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b525ee4-3cf7-5d76-a53d-685b40668c88" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449003} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "784695a4-512d-5fdd-8693-62a2ab3a9b61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447928} {:answer/id #uuid "cf7d3d75-f754-5646-a945-d3f52334331d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447929}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447929} :answer-field/id #uuid "028c41b0-ebc3-5901-b580-98c12b660e74" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449004} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "487f3bbf-a084-5508-9f38-062284a461b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447930} {:answer/id #uuid "2def3e0c-88ec-541d-aa1e-fc1120e9cd1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447931}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447930} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e422815-847c-5334-a438-2686d5ae65a2" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449005}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "727e9706-00b1-50a3-b21c-2eb79073afed" :question/math-academy-id "q-107534" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n- **II.** $[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}$\n- **III.** ${[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis true if and only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ So, the set is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]=2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ can't be written as a linear combination of \n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\nsince all these linear combinations result in matrices with the entry ${a}_{21}=0:$\n\n$$\na\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+b\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a & -a \\\\ 0 & 2b\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "91cfcf29-baa9-52fa-a889-0d9f790d046b"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486507 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486410 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b2c106c-830c-550f-9834-4e30ffa6398f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485925} {:answer/id #uuid "70454c64-8842-5c74-90d5-a65a027e02d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485957} {:answer/id #uuid "9b5a2ff6-779a-5eee-95b7-e7c15c73d9b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486043} {:answer/id #uuid "a2106cd2-d4c4-5485-bd50-b22dc45d0703" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486052} {:answer/id #uuid "d51926b0-1f7a-5517-b205-7d50c8ea1fd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486154}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485925} :answer-field/id #uuid "20236f24-a672-571d-8a2d-4eb8e53f4a42" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486249}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8e6a690a-1af9-54cd-96d8-6699f35c43cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-94865" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\{[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n\nII. $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\{[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\}$\n\nIII. $\\{[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis true if and only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ So, the set is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that all linear combinations of \n\n$$\n\\{[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\nresult in matrices with the entry ${a}_{21}=0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ can't be written as a linear combination of the set, it can't form a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} :step/id #uuid "b752cd7b-1282-56eb-b8ed-21c8bfb9825b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486493}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486522 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406883 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8bbdee2f-94bd-572f-91ae-a989e989cefc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454060} {:answer/id #uuid "ad6016de-8322-5611-abac-11c53498b5f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454061}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186454060} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b86c8c7-ce4a-5843-b124-fd92dc984de2" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454780} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e5061063-49ba-56ef-8537-f424f6dc8a16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454062} {:answer/id #uuid "710153ca-da8e-5423-9cbc-1f6713a46398" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454063}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186454063} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7647c92-3c6a-55d5-b2a1-537c5b726446" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454781} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0743a148-5538-58b8-be74-ca107d1c906d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454064} {:answer/id #uuid "f3c81ac2-78b7-59ca-8a48-01ff07cdd26a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454065}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186454064} :answer-field/id #uuid "ccfdb226-5087-511e-8a84-93a1b34f530a" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454782}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b3557614-de80-516a-bb07-7b50ae99a56a" :question/math-academy-id "q-80631" :question/problem "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ and $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$ are bases of ${R}^{3}$ and ${R}^{2}$, respectively. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${x,{b}_{2},{c}_{1}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{3}$\n- **II.** ${-2{b}_{3},3{b}_{1},5{b}_{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{3}$\n- **III.** ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2},x}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "Since $B$ and $C$ are bases, the following properties must hold:\n\n- $B$ and $C$ are maximally linearly independent sets in ${R}^{2}$ and ${R}^{3},$ respectively, and\n\n- $B$ spans ${R}^{2},$ while $C$ spans ${R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The set ${x,{b}_{2},{c}_{1}}$ is not a basis of ${R}^{3}$ because the set contains vectors from another vector space.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(-2{b}_{3})+{x}_{2}(3{b}_{1})+{x}_{3}(5{b}_{2}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2{x}_{1}){b}_{3}+(3{x}_{2}){b}_{1}+(5{x}_{3}){b}_{2} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, since ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ is linearly independent, gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, ${-2{b}_{3},3{b}_{1},5{b}_{2}}$ spans $V$ since any linear combination of vectors from $B$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${-2{b}_{3},3{b}_{1},5{b}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+{x}_{3}{b}_{3}=\\frac{{x}_{1}}{3}(3{b}_{1})+\\frac{{x}_{2}}{5}(5{b}_{2})+(-\\frac{{x}_{3}}{2})(-2{b}_{3})\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$, any vector of ${R}^{2},$ including the given vector $x,$ can be written as a linear combination of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ This means that ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2},x}$ is linearly dependent and therefore, cannot be a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "fc090e22-1e8a-579b-9615-543cf7ef59c3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486507}}] :activity/title "Bases in Abstract Vector Spaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485759}]}
